{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ביום הנחילו", :translit "b'yom hanchilo", :gloss "on the day he gives as an inheritance"} {:he "לא יוכל לבכר", :translit "lo yuchal levacher", :gloss "he may not give firstborn status"} {:he "כי אם בני בכורי ישראל", :translit "ki im b'ni v'chori yisra'el", :gloss "for they are My firstborn sons, Israel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The phrase 'on the day he gives as an inheritance' is read eschatologically as referring to the World to Come."}], :frame {:text "God declares Israel His firstborn at Exodus 4:22 — 'Israel is My son, My firstborn.' The Ba'al HaTurim reads the human law of inheritance as reflecting God's irrevocable designation of Israel as firstborn, which obtains fully in the time to come.", :sources ["Exodus 4:22"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ביום הנחילו את בניו", :translit "b'yom hanchilo et b'nav", :gloss "on the day he gives as an inheritance to his sons"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ביום הנחילו את בניו", :spells "מותו"}], :frame {:text "Rabbi Yehudah's ruling appears in a Talmudic discussion of visiting the gravely ill on Shabbat: three visitors who enter to check on a dying man — if it is still day, they may write a bill or render judgment; if night has fallen, they may write but may not render judgment (Gittin 67a). The word 'day' in the verse thus connects to the halakhic question of whether a deathbed gift distribution follows formal legal procedure, which requires daylight.", :sources ["Gittin 67a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את אשר יהיה לו", :translit "et asher yihyeh lo", :gloss "that which will be his"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "את אשר יהיה לו", :b "והבן נוטל בראוי כבמוחזק"}], :frame nil} {:quotes [{:he "את אשר יהיה לו", :translit "et asher yihyeh lo", :gloss "that which will be his"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "את אשר יהיה לו", :spells "תורה"}], :frame {:text "The Mishnah states that whoever stipulates against what is written in the Torah — his stipulation is void (Ketubot 56a, building on the principle at Bava Metzi'a 94a and codified in Mishneh Torah, Ishut 23:18). Ba'al HaTurim applies this rule to inheritance: a father cannot redirect the Torah's inheritance laws via stipulation, because the sofei-tevot of the verse itself spells 'Torah,' signaling the divine law's supremacy over any human condition.", :sources ["Ketubot 56a" "Bava Metzi'a 94a" "Mishneh Torah Ishut 23:18"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "The phrase 'on the day he gives as an inheritance' is read eschatologically as referring to the World to Come.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ביום הנחילו את בניו", :spells "מותו", :letters ["ם" "ו" "ת" "ו"], :by :selah, :order :in-order} {:kind :gematria, :a "את אשר יהיה לו", :a-value 968, :b "והבן נוטל בראוי כבמוחזק", :b-value 560, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "את אשר יהיה לו", :spells "תורה", :letters ["ת" "ר" "ה" "ו"], :by :selah, :order :bag}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:56.452681960Z", :model "glm-5.2"}